workedExamples.question_text:
Three empty containers are filled at the same constant volume rate. Their vertical cross-sections are shown. Sketch a water-height–time graph for each container. Label the axes.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (-0.5,2)--(-0.5,0)--(0.5,0)--(0.5,2);
\draw (2,2)--(2.6,0)--(3,0)--(3.6,2);
\draw (4.8,2)--(4.2,0)--(5.8,0)--(5.2,2);
\path (0,-0.4) node {(a)} (2.8,-0.4) node {(b)} (5,-0.4) node {(c)};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
For (a), the cross-section stays constant, so the height rises at a constant rate: draw a straight rising line.
For (b), the container widens upwards, so the height rises quickly at first, then more slowly: draw a rising curve that gets flatter.
For (c), the container narrows upwards, so the height rises slowly at first, then more quickly: draw a rising curve that gets steeper.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
