workedExamples.question_text:
An empty container has a narrow cylindrical lower section and an upper section that gradually widens. Its vertical cross-section is shown. Water enters at a constant volume rate. Sketch the water-depth–time graph through both sections.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (-1.2,3)--(-0.35,1.1)--(-0.35,0)--(0.35,0)--(0.35,1.1)--(1.2,3);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
In the cylindrical section, the depth rises at a constant rate: start with a steep straight line. In the widening section, the depth keeps rising but its rate decreases: continue with a curve that gets flatter. Join the pieces smoothly because the width is continuous at the join.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{rateblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->] (0,0)--(4.4,0);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,3.2);
\path (2.2,-0.4) node {Time} (-0.45,1.6) node[rotate=90] {Depth};
\draw[thick,rateblue] (0,0)--(0.5,1.1) .. controls (0.8,1.76) and (1.8,2.45) .. (3.8,2.9);
\draw[dashed] (0.5,0)--(0.5,1.1);
\path (0.5,-0.85) node {Upper section begins};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Initially the water surface is wide, so removing water lowers the depth slowly. As the surface becomes narrower, the same volume removed causes a larger depth drop. Draw a falling curve that becomes steeper and reaches zero when the container is empty.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
